Z||000000||HummelHund||09-02-2001||12:33 AM||||Well, my 84' SE's 2.5 is showing all the signs of worn rings: it smokes, it burns oil, and it's got a lot of blowby into the air cleaner. What sort of price will I be looking at if I get it rebuilt? What would the price be for a rebuilt replacement engine? Just ballpark figures would be fine, I know a few of you have been through this. And no, I'm not selling it to buy a V6, so all you GT/Formula/SE V6 people just stay out of this.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> <BR>Thanks!<BR><P>------------------<BR>Sean W.<BR>'84 SE 2M4 WS6, The "Buzz Bomb". (restoration project to become daily driver)<BR>84' SE 2M4 WS6 (Dad's Project)||||reg||5
Z||000001||sprnt96||09-02-2001||12:42 AM||||I JUST had my 84 duke mtr totally rebuilt because of a burnt piston/cracked head, it ran me approx $1400 but that included everything including a new cam as a lobe was worn..||||reg||1
Z||000002||BlackDragon||09-02-2001||12:54 AM||||im in the process of rebuilding mine, it is going to cost me about $500. thats with a polished crank, and reworked heads<P>------------------<BR>im looking for a inexpensive black standard fastback. does not have to run. does not have to look good. only the mid section needs to be dent free. the fenders,bumpers, dont have to be in good cond. faded paint is fine. anyone who has info, email me. grey interior would be nice but beggars cant be choosers||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroSoul||09-02-2001||07:47 AM||||Hey Hummel,<BR> Call Ed at Fiero Factory.  He will replace it with a new duke for about $2000, could be less depending on what you need.  Nice guy and he isn't far away.  About a 6 hour drive from us in Norff Alabama.||||reg||6
Z||000004||HummelHund||09-02-2001||01:58 PM||||I know of Ed Parks, he is an extremely nice guy. That's probably the route I'll be taking when it comes time for the rebuild, I need to make my required pilgrimage to the Fiero Factory anyway. Any tips on what sort of sacrifice I should bring? A Honda, or maybe an MR2?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>Sean W.<BR>'84 SE 2M4 WS6, The "Buzz Bomb". (restoration project to become daily driver)<BR>84' SE 2M4 WS6 (Dad's Project)||||reg||1
Z||000005||Avery Montembeault||09-03-2001||06:00 PM||||I spent around $500 bringing my 2.5 back from the dead, but my problems were different. My timing gear went, so I replaced that, crank gear, gaskets, filters, altenator, plugs, wires, sensors etc. i'm looking to convert though to a v6 and would be willing to part with my 2.5 for about what I put into it. its got almost 88k on it and is coming out of an 88 coupe||||reg||1
Z||000006||theogre||09-03-2001||06:13 PM||||Before sending a motor out, call around for prices on crate rebuilts. Jasper is one rebuilder that's been around a long time and has a good rep. There are others as well. Turnaround time on crate rebuilt is usually pretty short. There will be a core charge on most crates. You've got to send the old one back. It pays to find someone with local outlets so you don't have to pay for truck shipping.||||reg||1
